        "display_name": "Sector Confirmed-Range Breakout",
        "discipline": "breakout",
        "thesis": "A close that breaks above a confirmed multi-week swing high forces two groups to act, not just one: short sellers who sold into the old resistance are now underwater and mechanically buy to cover, and systematic trend/breakout funds (CTA-style N-day-high rules are a large, well-documented share of managed-futures and momentum flow) generate a fresh buy signal on the same event. Both are non-discretionary, price-triggered flows, which is why a break of an established range tends to keep going for a while rather than immediately mean-revert -- the buying is not opinion, it is a rule firing. The sma(200) trend filter restricts entries to sectors already in a primary uptrend, because a break of a local high inside a primary downtrend is disproportionately a bear-market rally, not a fresh trend. What I expect to go wrong: a grinding, broad-based bear (bear-2022-style) still produces some sector-level breakout signals -- sectors do not top in lockstep -- and those are precisely the trades that get overrun when the broader decline catches up, so I expect this card's worst window to be a bear regime, not a crash. See memo for the measured entry-frequency-by-window breakdown that grounds this.",
